{"sections":[],"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"metadata":{"title":"Graph","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"extension"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Graph","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P"}],"roleHeading":"Extended Protocol","role":"symbol","extendedModule":"GraphProtocols","modules":[{"name":"Planarity","relatedModules":["GraphProtocols"]}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Graph"}],"symbolKind":"extension","externalID":"s:e:s:14GraphProtocols0A0P9PlanarityE16_planarEdgeLimitSivp"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Planarity on undirected graphs. Self-loops and parallel edges never change an answer: the tests"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"run on the underlying simple graph, read once through the rows in index space. A directed"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"graph’s planarity is that of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"graph.undirectedView"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"extension"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Graph"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"anchor":"overview","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"isPlanar"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"planarEmbedding()"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"isOuterplanar"},{"type":"text","text":" run the left-right planarity test (de"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Fraysseix and Rosenstiehl, in Brandes’s formulation, 2009); "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"findKuratowskiSubgraph()"},{"type":"text","text":" runs"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Boyer and Myrvold’s edge-addition algorithm (2004), whose failure state isolates the witness."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"All are O(n + m), iterative, and deterministic: graphs with the same rows (the same vertices in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the same order, the same edge positions, the same incidence order) get the same embedding and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the same witness."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"topicSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Instance Properties","anchor":"Instance-Properties","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isOuterplanar","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/isPlanar"]},{"generated":true,"title":"Instance Methods","anchor":"Instance-Methods","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/findKuratowskiSubgraph()","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/planarEmbedding()"]}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity","doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols"]]},"references":{"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Graph":{"url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/graph","kind":"symbol","title":"Graph","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"Graph"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"Graph"}],"type":"topic","role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","abstract":[{"text":"An undirected graph: a finite set of vertices and a finite collection of undirected edges","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"between them. Kept apart from ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"DirectedGraph"},{"text":": an undirected graph is not a symmetric directed","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"one, and no type is both. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"directedView"},{"text":" views it as one when an algorithm needs arcs.","type":"text"}]},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/findKuratowskiSubgraph()":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Planarity\/documentation\/Planarity\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/findKuratowskiSubgraph()","type":"topic","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"findKuratowskiSubgraph","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"KuratowskiSubgraph","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:9Planarity18KuratowskiSubgraphV"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":">?","kind":"text"}],"abstract":[{"text":"A subdivision of K₅ or K₃,₃ made of the graph’s edges, or nil when the graph is planar: the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"witness of non-planarity (Boost’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"kuratowski_subgraph"},{"text":", JGraphT’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"getKuratowskiSubdivision()"},{"text":"). A dense graph is searched on its first 3n − 5 distinct pairs","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"only, which already hold one. O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/findkuratowskisubgraph()","title":"findKuratowskiSubgraph()"},"doc://Planarity/documentation/Planarity/GraphProtocols/Graph/isPlanar":{"url":"\/documentation\/planarity\/graphprotocols\/graph\/isplanar","type":"topic","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"isPlanar"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Bool","preciseId